<h1>Auto-Fix Engine</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Automatically extract hardcoded secrets to environment variables and rewrite source code in place.</p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How Auto-Fix Works</h2>

    <p>Gate's auto-fix engine rewrites source files using pattern-based text substitution, not an AST parser. This makes it fast and language-agnostic, but means rewrites follow predictable textual rules rather than semantic analysis. For each finding, Gate:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Derives an environment variable name from the rule, surrounding code context, or a SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE fallback.</li>
      <li>Replaces the hardcoded value in the source file with the language-appropriate env var lookup expression.</li>
      <li>Appends the variable and its value to <code>.env</code> (creating the file if it does not exist).</li>
      <li>Adds the variable name and a placeholder to <code>.env.example</code> so teammates know what to set.</li>
      <li>Injects a dotenv loader at the top of the entry file if one is not already present (JavaScript/TypeScript only).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Pattern-based, not AST-based.</strong> Auto-fix is designed for the common case: a string literal assigned to a variable. If your code has unusual string construction (concatenation, template literals, etc.), Gate will flag the finding but may not be able to rewrite it automatically. In that case, fix it manually.
    </div>

    <h2 id="commands">Commands</h2>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Command</th>
          <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>gate fix</td>
          <td>Run auto-fix on all findings in the current directory. Modifies files in place.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>gate fix --dry-run</td>
          <td>Show what would change without modifying any files.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>gate fix --undo</td>
          <td>Roll back the last auto-fix run for this repository using a stored snapshot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>gate fix [path]</td>
          <td>Fix only the specified file or directory.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>gate fix --rule [id]</td>
          <td>Fix only findings from the specified rule ID.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="language-support">Language Support</h2>

    <p>The table below shows how Gate rewrites each language and what expression replaces the hardcoded value.</p>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Language / Format</th>
          <th>Rewritten expression</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>JavaScript / TypeScript</td>
          <td>process.env.VAR_NAME</td>
          <td>Dotenv loader injected if not present</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Python</td>
          <td>os.environ["VAR_NAME"]</td>
          <td>import os injected at top of file if absent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Go</td>
          <td>os.Getenv("VAR_NAME")</td>
          <td>"os" added to import block if absent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Ruby</td>
          <td>ENV["VAR_NAME"]</td>
          <td>No import needed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Java</td>
          <td>System.getenv("VAR_NAME")</td>
          <td>No import needed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>YAML</td>
          <td>${VAR_NAME}</td>
          <td>Standard YAML env interpolation syntax</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Terraform</td>
          <td>var.var_name</td>
          <td>variable block appended to variables.tf</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dockerfile</td>
          <td>ARG VAR_NAME</td>
          <td>ENV instruction converted to ARG; value moved to build args</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>JSON</td>
          <td>(value extracted only)</td>
          <td>JSON has no env syntax; Gate extracts to .env, manual migration required</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>JavaScript / TypeScript detail</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// Before</span>
const stripe = <span class="kw">new</span> Stripe(<span class="str">"sk_live_51Hb3azLk...R4pM"</span>);

<span class="cmt">// After</span>
const stripe = <span class="kw">new</span> Stripe(process.env.<span class="val">STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</span>);</div>

    <h3>Python detail</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Before</span>
client = openai.OpenAI(api_key=<span class="str">"sk-proj-abc123..."</span>)

<span class="cmt"># After (import os injected at top of file)</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> os
client = openai.OpenAI(api_key=os.environ[<span class="str">"OPENAI_API_KEY"</span>])</div>

    <h3>Go detail</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// Before</span>
cfg := aws.Config{Region: <span class="str">"us-east-1"</span>, Token: <span class="str">"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"</span>}

<span class="cmt">// After ("os" added to imports)</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="str">"os"</span>
cfg := aws.Config{Region: <span class="str">"us-east-1"</span>, Token: os.Getenv(<span class="str">"AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID"</span>)}</div>

    <h3>Terraform detail</h3>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Before</span>
provider <span class="str">"aws"</span> {
  access_key = <span class="str">"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"</span>
}

<span class="cmt"># After</span>
provider <span class="str">"aws"</span> {
  access_key = var.aws_access_key
}

<span class="cmt"># Appended to variables.tf</span>
variable <span class="str">"aws_access_key"</span> {
  description = <span class="str">"AWS access key"</span>
  type        = string
  sensitive   = true
}</div>

    <h2 id="env-file-handling">.env File Handling</h2>

    <p>Gate writes extracted values to a <code>.env</code> file in the project root. The file is created if it does not exist. If it already exists, Gate appends new variables. Conflict resolution rules:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Variable not present</strong> -- appended as <code>VAR_NAME=value</code>.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Variable present with the same value</strong> -- no-op, nothing written.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Variable present with a different value</strong> -- new variable written as <code>VAR_NAME_NEW=value</code> with a comment noting the conflict. You resolve it manually.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># .env after gate fix</span>
<span class="val">STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</span>=sk_live_51Hb3azLk...R4pM
<span class="val">OPENAI_API_KEY</span>=sk-proj-abc123...

<span class="cmt"># Conflict: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY already existed with a different value</span>
<span class="cmt"># gate-conflict: resolve manually</span>
<span class="val">STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_NEW</span>=sk_live_different...</div>

    <h3>.env.example generation</h3>
    <p>Gate also writes or updates <code>.env.example</code> with placeholder values so teammates know which variables to set. The actual values are never written to <code>.env.example</code>.</p>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># .env.example after gate fix</span>
<span class="val">STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</span>=your_stripe_secret_key_here
<span class="val">OPENAI_API_KEY</span>=your_openai_api_key_here</div>

    <h2 id="dotenv-injection">Dotenv Loader Injection (JavaScript / TypeScript)</h2>

    <p>For JavaScript and TypeScript projects, Gate automatically adds a dotenv import at the top of the detected entry file if one is not already present. Gate detects the module format from <code>package.json</code>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">CommonJS</strong> (<code>"type"</code> absent or <code>"commonjs"</code>) -- injects <code>require('dotenv').config()</code>.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">ESM</strong> (<code>"type": "module"</code>) -- injects <code>import 'dotenv/config'</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// CJS injection (index.js / server.js)</span>
require(<span class="str">'dotenv'</span>).config();

<span class="cmt">// ESM injection (index.js with "type": "module")</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="str">'dotenv/config'</span>;</div>

    <p>Gate also ensures <code>dotenv</code> is in <code>package.json</code> dependencies (adding it if absent) and runs <code>npm install</code> silently in the background.</p>

    <h2 id="dry-run">Dry-Run Mode</h2>

    <p>Use <code>gate fix --dry-run</code> to preview all changes without writing any files. The output shows a unified diff of what would change in each affected file, plus the new <code>.env</code> entries that would be written.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --dry-run</span>

<span class="dim">  --- src/api/payments.ts</span>
<span class="dim">  +++ src/api/payments.ts (fixed)</span>
<span class="err">  - const stripe = new Stripe("sk_live_51Hb3azLk...R4pM");</span>
<span class="ok">  + const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);</span>

<span class="dim">  .env (would append):</span>
<span class="ok">  + STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_51Hb3azLk...R4pM</span>

<span class="dim">  1 file would be modified. 1 variable would be extracted.</span>
<span class="dim">  Run without --dry-run to apply.</span></div>

    <h2 id="undo">Undo</h2>

    <p>Before making any changes, Gate saves a snapshot of every file it will modify. Snapshots are stored in <code>~/.gate/snapshots/</code>, keyed by the sha256 of the repository root path. Gate retains up to 10 snapshots per repository; older ones are automatically pruned.</p>

    <p>To roll back the last fix run:</p>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --undo</span>

<span class="dim">  Restoring 2 files from snapshot taken 3 minutes ago...</span>
<span class="ok">  Restored src/api/payments.ts</span>
<span class="ok">  Restored .env</span>
<span class="dim">  Undo complete.</span></div>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Undo only rolls back source files.</strong> It does not undo changes to your Git staging area or any commits you made after running gate fix. If you committed the changes, use <code>git revert</code> instead.
    </div>

    <h2 id="pre-commit-hooks">Pre-Commit Hook Behavior</h2>

    <p>When Gate runs as a pre-commit hook and auto-fix is applied, Gate re-scans the fixed files. If the re-scan is clean, the fixed files are automatically re-staged so your commit includes the remediated code rather than the original.</p>

    <p>If the re-scan still finds issues (for example, a second secret in the same file that auto-fix cannot handle), the commit is blocked and you are shown the remaining findings.</p>

    <h2 id="env-var-naming">Env Var Name Derivation</h2>

    <p>Gate derives the environment variable name for each finding in this order:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Known mappings</strong> -- if the rule ID has a well-known canonical name (e.g., <code>stripe-secret-key</code> → <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code>), that name is used.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Code context</strong> -- Gate looks at the variable or key name in the surrounding code (e.g., <code>const apiKey = "..."</code> → <code>API_KEY</code>, <code>{"openai_key": "..."}</code> → <code>OPENAI_KEY</code>).</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE fallback</strong> -- if neither of the above yields a clean name, the rule ID is uppercased and dashes replaced with underscores (e.g., <code>generic-api-key</code> → <code>GENERIC_API_KEY</code>).</li>
    </ol>

    <p>In all cases, Gate deduplicates variable names within a single fix run. If two findings map to the same name but different values, the second gets a numeric suffix (<code>API_KEY_2</code>).</p>

